Close the security defects in the STOCK A2065 ethernet driver: the 4.2BSD
trailer RX arm (out-of-bounds read + mblk heap overflow, both reachable from
the wire), the unguarded b_cont dereferences in aenioctl() (kernel NULL
dereference reachable from any process that can open the device), and three
broken exits from aenioctl()'s switch that hand the SAME message to the shared
ACK epilogue twice (one double free, two use-after-handoff double putnext()s).

amiga/driver/aen/aen.c is the stock Amix 2.1 STREAMS/DLPI driver for the A2065
ethernet card (`en0` / `aen0`).  It is linked into EVERY Amix kernel -- aen/exp
is an unconditional entry in amiga/driver/Makefile's OBJ list -- so the defects
are live in emulation and on any stock install, whether or not the
machine has an A2065 fitted (the driver is present; only the board probe fails).

The first two were found in 2026-07 while auditing amix-z3660net's z3660eth, the
native Z3660 ethernet driver, which had inherited them verbatim from this file
(fixed there in z3660eth commits 54729bb and cd97e53).  The third group was found
by sweeping this switch afterwards; z3660eth does NOT inherit it (its
CLEAR_STATUS is an EINVAL stub and it has no AEN_SET_CONFIG analogue).  The LOW-
severity quality items also fixed in z3660eth are deliberately NOT ported: stock
aen is not ours to improve beyond safety.  Everything here is memory safety.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
[HIGH] the trailer RX arm -- toss_packet_up_stream()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Stock decodes the 4.2BSD trailer encapsulation for any received frame whose
ethertype falls in ETHERTYPE_TRAIL (0x1000) .. +ETHERTYPE_NTRAILER (0x1010):

    length = (sap - ETHERTYPE_TRAIL) * 512;                     /* up to 8192 */
    trailn = (*((unsigned short *)
                &packet[sizeof(ether_header_t) + length + ETH_TYPE_SIZE]) -
              ETH_TYPE_SIZE - ETH_LENGTH_SIZE);

`packet` is the driver's static `rxbuff[MAX_BUFFER_LENGTH]` -- 1518 bytes.  With
the maximum in-range ethertype 0x1010 the trailer-count word is read at offset
14 + 8192 + 2 = 8208, i.e. 6690 bytes (~6.7 KB) PAST the end of the buffer.
That is defect one: an out-of-bounds read of adjacent kernel BSS, driven purely
by two bytes an attacker puts in the ethertype field.

Defect two follows from it.  `trailn` is whatever those out-of-bounds bytes
happened to contain, minus 4 -- so it can be NEGATIVE.  The payload block is
then allocated as

    mp->b_cont = allocb(length + trailn, BPRI_MED)

while the copy that follows is unconditionally

    bcopy(&packet[sizeof(ether_header_t)], mp->b_cont->b_wptr, length)

so a negative `trailn` makes the destination mblk SMALLER than the copy: an
mblk heap overflow, again with attacker-influenced length.

The arm is dead code for legitimate traffic -- SVR4 IP never negotiates
trailers, aenopen() advertises IFF_NOTRAILERS, and interface bring-up is
`ifconfig en0 ... -trailers` -- but "unused" is not "unreachable": the decode
runs off the received ethertype alone, before any of that matters, so a single
crafted frame off the LAN reaches it.  It is therefore strictly worse than no
code, and it is DELETED rather than repaired.  Nothing on any network this
kernel will see speaks trailers.

Normal RX is byte-for-byte unchanged.  The guard is a two-way split on the
ethertype and only the taken arm is removed: every other ethertype (IP 0x0800,
ARP 0x0806, ...) already fell through to the else-arm, and that else-arm --
`length = count - ETH_CRC_LEN; trailn = 0;` -- is exactly what is now
unconditional.  With trailn == 0 the deleted `if (trailn)` bcopy block never
ran and allocb(length + trailn) == allocb(length), so both surviving statements
are unchanged in effect.  Confirmed by cross-disassembly of the compiled object
(m68k-cbm-sysv4 gcc 2.7.2.3, the aen Makefile's own CFLAGS): in the patched
toss_packet_up_stream() the range test (addiw #-4096 / cmpiw #16 / bhi), the
x512 shift (moveq #9 / asll) and the indexed out-of-bounds read
(movew %a1@(0x10,%d6:l)) are all gone, and the entry path falls straight into
the former else-arm (movel count / subql #4).  Every other function in the
object is instruction-for-instruction identical.

Trailer-tagged frames now take the ordinary delivery path: `sap` keeps its
0x1000..0x1010 value instead of being replaced by the encapsulated type, no
stream is bound to those SAPs, and the frame is simply not delivered -- correct
handling for an encapsulation this stack never negotiated.  One visible
consequence: aen_status.trailers is now always 0.  The field is KEPT because it
is part of aen_status_t, which AEN_GET_STATUS copies out to userland; removing
it would change a userland-visible struct layout for no benefit.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
[MED] the ioctl NULL dereference -- aenioctl()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Five sites opened by dereferencing the ioctl's data block with no check at all:

    caddr_t arg = *(caddr_t *)mp->b_cont->b_rptr;    /* AEN_GET_STATUS,
                                                        AEN_GET_CONFIG,
                                                        AEN_NUMBER_OF_BOARDS */
    creq->cq_addr = *(caddr_t *)mp->b_cont->b_rptr;  /* AEN_SET_CONFIG,
                                                        TRANSPARENT arm */
    (void)pullupmsg(mp->b_cont, -1);                 /* AEN_SET_CONFIG,
                                                        non-TRANSPARENT arm */

An M_IOCTL legitimately arrives with b_cont == NULL: that is precisely what a
non-TRANSPARENT I_STR ioctl carrying zero bytes of data looks like.  For the
first three cases the dereference happens BEFORE any TRANSPARENT test, so

    struct strioctl s;
    s.ic_cmd = AEN_GET_STATUS; s.ic_len = 0; s.ic_dp = 0; s.ic_timout = 0;
    ioctl(fd, I_STR, &s);

is a kernel NULL dereference -- a panic -- from any process that can open the
device.  A b_cont that is present but shorter than a pointer would likewise be
over-read.  (The AEN_SET_CONFIG cq_addr site sits inside its TRANSPARENT branch,
so it is not reachable that way; it is guarded here anyway, at zero behavioural
cost, so the file has one uniform rule.)

The fifth site is the same defect wearing a disguise, and it is NOT on a
TRANSPARENT path.  The non-TRANSPARENT AEN_SET_CONFIG arm reads

    (void)pullupmsg(mp->b_cont, -1);
    if (!mp->b_cont || blklen(mp->b_cont) != sizeof(*aen_config))

-- the NULL test is written one line too late.  pullupmsg() dereferences its
argument immediately (disassembly of the staged io/exp: `moveal %fp@(8),%a5`
then `tstl %a5@(8)`, no NULL check anywhere), so on exactly the message shape
the next line exists to reject, the panic has already happened and the test is
unreachable.  The test is simply moved ahead of the call.

Fixed with two new statics that follow the shapes already used in this file:

  aen_ioc_nak()   the single NAK path, byte-identical in effect to the NAK
      stanza aenioctl()'s own `default:` case already used (M_IOCNAK,
      freemsg(unlinkb(mp)), ioc_count = ioc_rval = 0, ioc_error = err,
      putnext(RD(q), mp)).
  aen_ioc_arg()   fetches the user address only when the message is the
      M_IOCTL+TRANSPARENT case that actually USES it -- the same condition as
      each use site, which also avoids reading ioc_count out of an M_IOCDATA
      message, where that block is really a struct copyresp.  It checks b_cont
      is non-NULL and at least sizeof(caddr_t) long, and reports failure so the
      caller can NAK.  For the non-TRANSPARENT case there is no user address to
      fetch, so it yields 0 and succeeds -- which is what makes b_cont == NULL
      safe rather than fatal.

All five sites now NAK EINVAL on a malformed message instead of panicking.
Behaviour for well-formed ioctls is unchanged: for a TRANSPARENT ioctl with a
valid argument the helper returns exactly the value the open-coded dereference
produced, and for every other message shape `arg` was never used.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
[HIGH] three broken exits from the switch -- aenioctl()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

aenioctl() ends in a shared ACK epilogue that every `break` in its switch falls
into:

    mp->b_datap->db_type = M_IOCACK;
    freemsg(unlinkb(mp));
    iocbp->ioc_count = 0; iocbp->ioc_rval = 0; iocbp->ioc_error = 0;
    putnext(RD(q), mp);

So `break` means "I have NOT disposed of mp; ACK it for me", and any case that
has already freed the data block, or already handed the whole message upstream,
must `return` instead.  Three did not.  All three are memory-safety bugs, not
protocol nits, and all three are reachable from userland.

 1. AEN_CLEAR_STATUS -- DOUBLE FREE.  The case ended

        freemsg(mp->b_cont);
        break;

    which frees the data block but leaves mp->b_cont still pointing at it.
    unlinkb() in the epilogue then returns that same stale pointer (disassembly:
    it loads b_cont from offset 8, clears the field, returns the old value), and
    freemsg() frees it a second time.  Any process that can open the device
    triggers it with one I_STR carrying a non-zero-length payload -- the payload
    is what creates the b_cont this case frees.  Fixed by making the free
    detach as it goes, `freemsg(unlinkb(mp));`, which is this file's own idiom
    (six existing sites) and leaves the epilogue a proven no-op: freemsg() NULL-
    tests its argument at entry (`tstl %a0 / beq`).  Deliberately NOT routed
    through aen_ioc_nak(): this case SUCCEEDS and must ACK, and it takes no
    argument, so neither helper fits it.

 2. AEN_SET_CONFIG, malformed non-TRANSPARENT message -- DOUBLE PUTNEXT.  The
    arm built an M_IOCNAK by hand, called putnext(RD(q), mp), and then `break`,
    so the epilogue re-typed the message to M_IOCACK, unlinkb()ed and freemsg()ed
    it, and putnext()ed it a SECOND time -- after the stream head had already
    taken ownership of it and freed it.  Rewritten as
    `aen_ioc_nak(q, mp, EINVAL); return;`, which is the same NAK the arm was
    hand-rolling (plus the ioc_rval = 0 and the data-block drop the file's other
    NAK sites do), with the exit corrected.

 3. AEN_SET_CONFIG, success -- DOUBLE PUTNEXT, on the MAINLINE path.  The arm
    unlinkb()s and freeb()s the data block, putnext()es its M_IOCACK, then reuses
    `mp` as scratch for the DL_BIND_ACK / DL_INFO_ACK messages it qreply()s round
    the AEN_MAXDEV loop -- and then `break`s into the epilogue, which writes
    db_type into, unlinkb()s, freemsg()es and re-putnext()es whichever of those
    already-handed-off messages `mp` last pointed at.  Changed to `return`.

    This one is not a corner: a TRANSPARENT ioctl(fd, AEN_SET_CONFIG, &cfg)
    comes back as the M_IOCDATA leg of the copyin, which is not M_IOCTL, so it
    takes this same arm.  Every successful AEN_SET_CONFIG, transparent or not,
    ended in a use-after-free plus a double putnext of one mblk -- precisely the
    shape that corrupts the STREAMS freelist and surfaces later, far from here,
    as an "out of blocks" wedge.

The three cases that still `break` (AEN_CLEAR_STATUS, and the non-TRANSPARENT
tails of AEN_GET_STATUS / AEN_GET_CONFIG / AEN_NUMBER_OF_BOARDS) all reach the
epilogue holding a message they have not disposed of, which is what it is for.
Proof that the sweep is complete and surgical: in the compiled object the number
of branches into the epilogue drops from 10 to 8, the two that disappear are
exactly the two `braw` sites above, and the remaining eight are unmoved.

(Not fixed, because it is a functional wart and not a safety defect: those three
non-TRANSPARENT tails build a reply block into b_cont and then let the epilogue
free it and zero ioc_count, so a non-TRANSPARENT I_STR AEN_GET_STATUS returns no
data.  Stock aen is not ours to improve beyond safety.)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Verified: host cross-build only (m68k-cbm-sysv4 gcc 2.7.2.3, the aen Makefile's
CFLAGS + -D__STDC__=0, staged /usr/sys sysroot).  Compiles clean with
diagnostics identical to the pre-patch baseline apart from the line numbers this
patch shifts, and an unchanged `nm -u` set (26 symbols).  Splitting the object
per function, aenioctl() and toss_packet_up_stream() differ, aen_ioc_arg() and
aen_ioc_nak() are new, and every other function is instruction-for-instruction
identical.  Not yet validated at run time: no emulated or real-hardware RX soak or
ioctl exercise has been run.

